ner, get an endpoint, scale from zero to thousands of instances and back down — across CPU and GPU.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em">On a hyperscaler, the serverless layer is a product someone else built. Here you build it end to end: the scheduler that decides where a container lands, the routing path a request takes, the machinery that turns a scaled-to-zero service into a warm instance before a customer notices. All of it runs on metal, network, and datacenters we own.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em">That tension is the product: scale-to-zero economics with cold starts in milliseconds, autoscaling that absorbs a launch spike without a config change, GPUs scheduled onto containers the way other platforms schedule CPU. The customers betting on this layer are the developers building the next generation of AI software on European infrastructure.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>WHAT YOU WILL DO</strong></p><p style="min-height:1.5em">- Build the container platform end to end: image delivery, placement, request routing, scale-up, scale-to-zero — across CPU and GPU.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em">- Make cold starts a solved problem: snapshotting, prewarming, image-layer caching, whatever it takes for scale-from-zero to feel instant.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em">- Treat AI workloads as the normal case: GPU-backed containers, streaming responses, long-lived connections, MCP servers that cost almost nothing while idle.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em">- Write Go and own the architecture and the hardest implementation decisions.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em">- Partner with Product Managers to turn platform decisions into a product developers want to deploy on.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>WHAT WE'RE LOOKING FOR</strong></p><p style="min-height:1.5em">- You have shipped production systems in Go. Not dabbled. Shipped.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em">- You have built container or workload platforms — orchestration, scheduling, isolation, request routing — not jus