v's day-to-day social presence on X and LinkedIn: product updates, customer wins, blog amplifications, conference content, and reactive posts on what's happening in the field.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Run the Proteinbase community day-to-day: newsletter, competition updates, designer spotlights, and the Slack where participants and contributors gather.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Own the participant-facing side of our open competitions and hackathons, from applicant communication and rules through to judging logistics and post-event recaps. Turn each event into a public artifact (leaderboard, dataset, blog post).</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Triage inbound community and customer messages, routing deeper technical questions to the right person on the team.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Coordinate with the founders and the science team on what to post, what to amplify, and what to hold.</p></li></ul><div style="min-height:1.2em;margin-top:0px;margin-bottom:0px"> </div><h2>What we're looking for</h2><ul style="min-height:1.5em"><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">1-3 years running social, content, or community for a technical company, a science-adjacent media brand, an event series, or a community platform. Self-built audiences and adjacent experience count equally.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">A strong, distinctive writing voice. You move between LinkedIn announcements, customer replies, and community posts without defaulting to generic AI phrasing.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Genuine curiosity about biology and AI. No science degree required, but you should be able to follow technical conversations in protein design and AI without needing them explained.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Highly organized. You'll juggle multiple channels, partnerships, and ongoing threads simultaneously, and build the systems to track them.</p></li><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Comfortable with ambiguity. There